// Book Three, Chapter 15 (session 36). The last days of August 1792 and the night of the second to the third of September. The convent, the Prince's crossing, the lists, the priest in the cellar, and the Abbaye.
// Opens on the end of August (no phase), then one night phase of three actions out of four (the night of the first to the second), then the dusk and the day told, then a fixed closing scene at the Abbaye that every player plays.
// Ends with rest() and => next. Reads b3.nationals, b3.brezac, b3.garnier, b3.file, b3.palace, b3.slate, b3.faubourg, b3.cadets_home and b3.baptiste. Chapter 16 should read hunger, standing and secrecy as this chapter left them.
@chapter b3c15
@title The Second of September
@book crown-of-ashes
@version 2

# start
## The last of August
$ b3.carmes = "watched"
$ b3.crossing = "left"
$ b3.arrests = "kept"
$ b3.priest = "left"
$ b3.abbaye = "stayed"
~> evening
?if must_feed()
  ~> hunger
?end
---
$ do phase("night", 3)
It is the night of the first of September, a Saturday, and a little after ten. There are four things that want doing by your own reckoning, and time for three.
-> night

# evening
## The barriers
Longwy fell on the twenty third. It was a small place, and it gave itself up in a day, and the news was a week reaching Paris, and it came as a thing comes that has been expected for a long time and has been got ready for in all the wrong ways. Verdun is besieged. It is eighty leagues. A good cavalry could be at the gates in a week, and the whole of Paris knows the figure, and says it over, in the shops, as people say over the price of bread.
The barriers have been shut since the twenty eighth. On the twenty ninth and thirtieth the Commune went through the houses, by night, with torches, looking for arms and for priests and for anybody who had not been seen to be afraid, and took three thousand, and put them in the Abbaye, and the Conciergerie, and La Force, and the Châtelet, and the old convent in the rue de Vaugirard, where the priests who would not take the oath have been kept since the spring. It was very well done. It was done on a list. A man who has a list is a man who is very much comforted.
?if b3.garnier == "turned"
  The Inspector's name is on one of them. It was written, you gather, by his own clerk, in a very neat hand.
?end
There is a good deal of talk of the prisons. It is said, in the cafés, with a note of impatience, that it is a poor thing to send the best of the city to the frontier, with the pikes, and leave behind it, in the gaols, three thousand persons who would be glad to open the doors to the Prussians. It is not said by a particular man. It is said by the weather.
The shop has a quiet that you do not like. Étienne is in his bed. Baptiste is out. Adélaïde has been moving through the house all week with a basin and a cloth and her face very steady, and has twice been seen, at the head of the cellar stair, listening, with her hand on the rail.
?if b3.slate == "told"
  The Prince is not in the Hôtel de Saint-Aubin. He is in the third cellar. Ottaviano says that he has not spoken since the Tuesday, except once, to say that it was his own hand, and that he was obliged to you.
?elif b3.slate == "rubbed"
  There is a stain of white dust on the cuff of the coat that you wore to the cellar. You have not been able to get it out. It has the look, in a certain light, of a letter.
?else
  You have the line by heart. You have had it, three weeks, like a coin in the mouth. The second of September is to be a Sunday. It is a Sunday.
?end
<~

# hunger
## The close night
It is the shut barriers. They have put the smell of the whole city into a box, and shut it, and the box is full of people who are afraid. In the rue de la Harpe a watchman, on a doorstep, with a pike across his knees, who has been told to stop everybody and who is asleep, snores, with his throat bare, quite easy to reach.
* Take it from the watchman, and put the pike back across his knees
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  He does not wake. He will say, in the morning, to a sergeant, that it was the heat. You put the pike straight, and it is a ridiculous thing to have done, and you are glad of it.
* Go down to the river, to the horses of the water carriers, and take it there
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  They stand in the dark in a line along the quay, with their heads down, patient and enormous. The big gray, who is nearest, lets you. It is not what the blood wants. It is a very honest way of getting it.
* Wait, and do not feed
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a thing that can be done. The night is long. You find that you have begun to count the doors that are shut.
<~

# convent
## The rue de Vaugirard
It is the old house of the Carmelites. It has a long gray front on the street, with a gate, and a court, and behind it a garden with a wall round it, and a chapel with a small dome, and under the chapel a crypt, that is dry, and very deep, and is the best cellar on the left bank of the river.
?if b3.nationals == "bought"
  The deed is in a drawer in the counting room, in Adélaïde's name. It is a quiet house. She has let the cells to a society of widows, and put a bench in the garden, and there is a smell of cabbage and wet linen. The gate is on the latch, and the porter, who is eighty, looks at you with great affection and does not get up.
?elif b3.nationals == "lent"
  It is the district's hospital, as you arranged it, with forty beds in the cells, and a small stove in the chapel. There is a surgeon asleep on a bench in the passage with his coat over his face, and a sister, who has not yet been told that she is no longer a sister, going round with a candle, quietly, bed by bed.
?else
  It is a prison. There is a sentry at the gate, a National Guard of fifty, with a red face and a pipe, who is quite amiable, and a turnkey, and in the cells, behind the old doors, a hundred and fifty priests who would not take the oath, sitting on the straw. They have been singing a very little. It is the sort of singing that is done to keep an hour in its place.
?end
You go down by the passage behind the sacristy, to the crypt. They are there, four of them, in the dark, on pallets, in their clothes, in the cold, among the stone coffins of nuns who have been dead two hundred years. They have not been sheltered or sent. They have wanted a deep place and a quiet one, and a place that was nobody's, and they found it. Clemence has sat up. She is in the man's coat. She says that there are three that she would give her life for and one that she would not, and that she has not yet decided which is which.
She says that the Commune has been in the garden twice this week, looking at the wall.
It is a quarter past one. It is a Sunday, very nearly, and the line on the slate is in your head as plain as the lamp. You know what is to be done in this house. You do not know whether you are able to make it not be done. It is a thing that you can do for one of two parties, and the dawn is five hours off.
* Take the four out before the day, by the passage to the garden, and leave the house as it is
  $ b3.carmes = "cadets"
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ bond.clemence += 2
  $ do chart("paris.rebels")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  It is done in a quarter of an hour. You are not gentle about it. You say that there is a thing coming that you cannot stop, and that it is not a thing that they will be able to get out of, once the doors are open, and that they are to come. Clemence looks at you for a good while, with her head on one side. She says, at the end, that she thought you would say that. She says that it is not at all what she would have said. They go up through the garden, in the dark, in a line, between the beds of lettuce, to a cart that Adélaïde's carter has left at the end of the lane, under a tarpaulin, and the cart goes away down the rue du Cherche-Midi, quite slowly, on a horse that is half asleep. You stand in the garden. In the cells, behind the old doors, somebody has begun to sing again, very low. You do not go in.
* Open the gate at three, and tell the priests that they may go
  $ b3.carmes = "priests"
  $ humanity += 3
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ standing -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  It is a thing that is not in the least difficult. You have the porter's keys, or the turnkey's, or the sister's, and you go from door to door in the old passage, in the lamplight, and say in a low voice what you say. Many of them do not believe it. Some of them are afraid of a trap, and some are not able to walk. A very old man, with a face like a seal, a bishop, who says that he is the Archbishop of Arles, thanks you for your trouble, and says that his place is with his people, and goes back and sits on the straw. At three you open the gate. About thirty walk out. They go in twos and threes, in the dark, with their hats pulled down, with that air of a person who is leaving church in the middle of the sermon. The sentry is asleep. The crypt, behind you, you do not go near. You have said that it is not possible, and you know that it was a choice.
* Stand in the garden, and do neither
  $ b3.carmes = "watched"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ bond.clemence -= 2
  $ do chart("paris.rebels")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You say that you will not do either. You are not sure why. You say that there is a thing that you want to see, and then you cannot tell what it is, and you stand a long time under the old pear tree, in the cold, listening to the hundred and fifty at their psalm, until it is the hour that you must go. Clemence has not spoken. She has not said that she will go if you will not. She says, as you go, in the voice of a woman who does not expect to be answered, that you might at least have said what you meant to do.
<~

# prince
## The plain coach
He is in the hall, in a gray traveling cloak, with a small trunk and a sword. It is the first time that you have seen him in the hall. The candles are out. The great doors are open, and in the court there is a plain coach, with the blinds drawn, and Ottaviano on the box, with his hat low, and the horses stamping.
?if b3.slate == "told"
  He says that it is quite right. He says that it is the thing that was written, and that he has made up his mind to it, as a man does who has been told by his physician what he must eat. He says that it is a very great relief. He says that he does not intend to see it.
?elif b3.slate == "rubbed"
  He says that you rubbed it out, and that it came again, and that he has been thinking about that, in the cellar, for three weeks. He says that he has decided to go. He says that he cannot say whether it is obedience or a form of revenge.
?else
  He says that he is going across the river. He says that it is to be a quiet Sunday, and that he finds that he has a wish to be in the Marais. He does not say why. He does not look at you while he says it, and it is not at all a kind of lie that he has the habit of.
?end
There is no one else in the house but the pale woman in gray, who sits on the stair with her hands in her lap.
?if b3.brezac == "held"
  Brézac is not here. He has been in the Rue de Vaugirard since midnight, he says, at the Prince's own word, and Ottaviano has been told to say that it was not an order.
?elif b3.brezac == "rode"
  Brézac has been out all night. He came in, at midnight, and was sick, in the great hall, quite quietly, in a corner, like a very old dog.
?end
* Ask him to put the court's eleven at the convent gate, and stay
  $ b3.crossing = "pressed"
  $ standing += 1
  $ defiance += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ bond.ottaviano += 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You say that it is not a thing that he may do, and that there is a house with a hundred and fifty men in it, and four of his own, and that there are eleven in this hall who would stand at a gate if he said it. He listens quite politely, with his head bent. At the end, he says that it is the first time in four hundred years that anyone has asked him for the sword of the house in order to use it on behalf of mortals. He says that he is not able. He says that he is not sure that it is a kindness to be asked. He goes out to the coach. But you have seen the pale woman, on the stair, put up her head.
* See him over the river, in the coach, with the blind drawn
  $ b3.crossing = "crossed"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ standing -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ bond.ottaviano += 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You get in. It is as dark as a box, and smells of leather and cold and of some sort of herb. He does not speak until the Pont Neuf. He says then, in the dark, that you are very good. He says that it is a thing that he has noticed, that you are always a little too good for the occasion. He says it without any malice. At the other side, at the Quai de la Mégisserie, he puts his hand out of the window to take the air, and you see, in the lamp of the bridge, that it is perfectly white, and quite steady, and that it is holding a handkerchief.
* Let him go, and do not follow
  $ b3.crossing = "left"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You stand in the court and watch the coach go out under the arch. It does not hurry. It goes out into the street at a walk, with its blinds, and turns, and is gone. Ottaviano, on the box, does not look round. The pale woman has come down the stair, and stands beside you, in the empty hall, with her hands folded, and says, after a long while, in a voice that is the voice of a very young person, whether it is wicked to be glad.
<~

# arrests
## The Hôtel de Ville
It is the Commune's, and it is a great ugly house with a hundred windows, all lit, and a sentry at every door who has been told to let in persons with a piece of paper. They are in the Surveillance Committee, in a long low room, under a bust of Brutus, at a table that is littered with lists. There are three clerks. There is a smell of candle grease and sour wine. They are not at all sinister. They are men who have worked a very long time at a very small salary, and who have been told, today, that they are the nation.
?if b3.garnier == "bargained"
  You have the Section's papers, the ones you had of the Inspector. It is a small thing. They are well made, and the youngest of the clerks, a flat-faced young man with ink to his elbows, reads your name, and looks again, and does not say anything about it.
?elif b3.garnier == "sheltered"
  The Inspector's old clerk is on the third desk. He has the look of a man who has been sent for, and does not know why. He sees you, and puts down his pen, and does not take it up for some time.
?else
  There is a sheet on the table in the Inspector's own hand. It is a list of eleven names, in the faubourg Saint-Germain, with a mark against each one that says only, very plainly, that they are dangerous.
?end
?if b3.file == "kept"
  You have the file that you kept, the lever, the thin buff folder that Garnier would have burned. It is a thing that has a use. You put it in your coat.
?end
There are five hundred names, on the long list of the Abbaye. There are a hundred and fifty on the list for the rue de Vaugirard. It is a thing that is done very quickly, at a quarter past two, by a very small man at a very big table, who reads them aloud with a pencil.
$ _mp_n = 0
$ _mp_rank = 0
:mp_more_1
* Go by the back stair to the families, and warn them [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 3
    $ b3.arrests = "warned"
    $ _mp_rank = 3
  ?end
  $ humanity += 2
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You copy out eleven names. You go to the houses. It is a thing that takes two hours and the whole of your ingenuity, and a great deal of knocking at doors that open on a chain. You say, to a woman in a nightcap, and to a man with a candle, and to a child in a shirt who is the only one to answer, that it will be well to be out of the house by the morning, and out of the quarter, and out of Paris if there is a way. Some do not believe you. Some of them are, by the morning, gone. It is not very much. It is a thing that you can say that you did.
* Pay a clerk to strike six names from the list [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ b3.arrests = "struck"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ do earn(-3)
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing += 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  It is a matter of a purse, and a flat-faced young man, and a cup of wine at the Café de la Régence, where you are two gentlemen of leisure. He does it neatly. He draws a line through the names with a very ordinary pen, and puts in the margin, in a very clerkly hand, that they have been released on the order of a Section. It is a thing that is done, he says, every day. You do not ask about the other four hundred and ninety.
* Copy the list, and say nothing of it [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 1
    $ b3.arrests = "kept"
    $ _mp_rank = 1
  ?end
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  It takes you the better part of an hour, in a corner, in a cold light, with a borrowed pen. It is a list of the whole of the prisoners of Paris, with the reason for each. It is the sort of thing that a person who would one day be asked, in the course of a long century, for the names of the dead, would be very glad to have. You put it in your coat. You do not look at the clerks. They do not look at you.
* That is the whole of it ?if _mp_n > 0
  -> :mp_done_1
-> :mp_more_1
:mp_done_1
