# shop
## The cellar stair
The shop is dark, and the yard is dark, and the old Swiss press has a sheet over it, like a piece of furniture in a house that is shut for the summer. There is a light under the counting room door. Adélaïde is at the foot of the cellar stair, in a wrapper, with her hair plaited, and a candle in a saucer. She has been waiting. She does not say good evening. She says that there is a person in the cellar.
He is in the old wine room, among the casks, on a pallet, with a book. He is a man of sixty or so, in an old black coat, and a shabby stock, with a round bald head, and a pair of steel spectacles on the end of his nose, who looks at you with a sort of mild, ironic benevolence, as at a thing that might one day be explained to him. He is the abbé who married her. He has been there since the tenth of August. He has never had the oath, and has not said what he thinks of it.
He says that it is very good of her. He says it as if to someone not in the room. He says that he is under no illusion. He says that a man in his position is something of a trouble, and that he would not wish to be the cause of any, and that he has been wondering, in his cellar, since Wednesday, with a certain amount of curiosity, whether he ought to give himself up.
Adélaïde stands very straight. She does not look at him. She says, to you, in a small clear voice, that she has told him that he is not to be thought of, and that she would like to know who has the right to say so.
* Hide him in the quarry under the shop, where the Cadets slept
  $ b3.priest = "hid"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  $ bond.adelaide += 2
  $ do chart("paris.presses")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You carry him down yourself, in your arms, as one does a very large child, because he cannot manage the ladder, and he says that it is most undignified, and that he is obliged. The old gallery is dry, and low, and smells of stone and of the sea. You put him in the dry chamber, the one the size of a chapel, among the bones. He looks about him with great interest. He says that it is the best sermon that he has ever been privileged to hear, and that it is a pity that it is not his own. Adélaïde lights the lamp. She sits on the step. They talk, as you go up, very low, about the price of wine.
* Put him on the Orléans coach, at a price
  $ b3.priest = "sent"
  $ do earn(-2)
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing += 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ bond.adelaide += 1
  $ do chart("paris.presses")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  It is a matter of a coachman, who is a cousin of the carter, and a passport that has the name of Baptiste's dead schoolfellow on it, and a bag. The barriers are shut. The coachman says that he knows a gate. You take the old man down to the rue du Faubourg Saint-Jacques, at four, in the dark, and put him in, among the sleeping persons, with his book. He thanks you, from the window, with great courtesy. He says that he will pray for you, if it is not an impertinence. It is a thing that you do not know how to answer.
* Let him go back to his house, as his conscience bids him
  $ b3.priest = "left"
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ bond.adelaide -= 2
  $ do chart("paris.presses")
  $ do discover("the_second_of_september")
  You say that it is a matter for himself. You say it in the voice that you have learned for it, kind and quite without hope, and you are aware, as you say it, of Adélaïde's face, turning, in the candlelight, like a leaf in water. He is satisfied. He says that it is a very fair thing to say. He puts on his hat, and kisses her hand, and goes up the stair into the court, and out. It is a quarter to four. He has a long way to walk, in the dark, to the rue de Vaugirard, and he is quite old, and does not hurry.
