# morning
## The Sunday
You are in the haven before the sun, with the stone over you, and you hear it, as you have heard every great day of this century, only as a noise through the rock. It begins at about two. It is a gun. It is the alarm gun on the Pont Neuf, a single dry flat thump, and then, after a pause that is exactly as long as you would expect it to be, another. After the guns, the tocsin, and after the tocsin, a long confused rolling sound, that is made of a great many feet, and a great many drums, and a great many voices that are saying the same few words.
It is told you at dusk, by Adélaïde, who has not come down the stair. She has sent the old porter's boy, with the tray, and a note. The note is in her clear plain hand, and is not signed, and it is very short.
It says that the first carriages left the Hôtel de Ville at about half past two, with twenty priests in them, for the Abbaye. It says that the mob caught them in the rue de Buci, and that they were killed at the gate, one by one, as they got down. It says that the thing was done, in the Abbaye, in a room, at a table, by a man named Maillard, in a gray coat, with a sword, who sits with a book and a lamp and judges them by the hour, and that it is not yet done. It says that the gates of the Carmes were opened at four. It says that it was done in the garden. It says that there were a hundred and fifteen. It says that the Archbishop of Arles, who was old, and lame, was the first of those who were killed on the steps of the chapel, and that they were all of them very quiet.
?if b3.carmes == "priests"
  It says that there were a good many fewer than there might have been. It says that the porter has been telling it at the baker's, in a voice of great astonishment: that on the night before, a gentleman opened the gate. She has underlined the word gentleman. She has not said which.
?elif b3.carmes == "cadets"
  It says that the cart came to the shop at half past three, in the dark, and that there were four persons in it, under a tarpaulin, and that one of them, a young woman in a man's coat, got down in the yard and was sick, and then stood, quite still, with her back to the wall, and would not be touched.
?else
  It says that the crypt was opened at about five. It says that there was a good deal of light. It says that the thing that is told in the quarter is a very odd one: that the mob, which had come for priests, broke into the cellar under the chapel, which was a quite empty cellar, and fled out of it, with a cry, and would not go in again. It is not said what they saw. The carter, who went for the bodies, has said that there was a great deal of ash.
?end
?if b3.carmes == "priests"
  It says that the crypt was not opened until five, and that when it was, there was a good deal of light, and that two of the four who slept there did not come out, and that the third got Clemence through the garden, in a sack, and is not able to say how.
?end
?if b3.carmes != "cadets"
  Clemence has come in at the yard door at about nine, with the marks of it on her hands and neck, dragged out of the crypt by the third of them, at the last, through the garden, in a sack. She has not said how she lived. She is in the attic, with the shutters nailed, and has asked that nobody speak to her but you.
?end
?if b3.priest == "left"
  The abbé walked to the rue de Vaugirard on the Sunday morning, in his old black coat, with his book, and gave himself up at the gate. He is not among those who are returned. It is not a thing that Adélaïde has written. It is a thing that is in the very straight line of her pen.
?elif b3.priest == "hid"
  The abbé is in the quarry, with a lamp. Adélaïde has been down with soup, every hour, and has not said what she has heard.
?elif b3.priest == "sent"
  A note came from Étampes at noon, in the old man's own hand, to say that he has arrived, and that there is no cause for anxiety. It is the one piece of good news in the house.
?end
?if b3.nationals == "bought"
  It is a house that stands in her name, and she does not know whether she may go into it again.
?elif b3.nationals == "lent"
  The forty beds were carried out, on the Saturday, by the surgeon and the sister, into the street, and put in a cart. It is the one thing in the whole matter that anyone has been able to say was foreseen.
?end
It is nearly eight. The drums are still going. In the rue Saint-Jacques there is a smell of smoke, and of wet stone, and somewhere a woman is singing a child to sleep in a high flat voice, with a great deal of care.
-> abbaye

# abbaye
## The table
It is a quarter to ten. The rue de Bussy is full of people, and of lanterns, and of a heavy sweetish smell, and the gate of the Abbaye is open, in the wall, in the light of a dozen torches, with a pile of straw before it, which is very red. It is not a thing that you will describe. You go in by the guard room. Nobody stops you. Everybody is a little drunk, and very polite, and a man in a leather apron offers you a cup of wine, which is a thing that you take, and do not drink.
In the court of the prison, in the lamplight, at a table with a green cloth, under the arch of a stair, there are twelve men. They are sitting in chairs. There is a lamp on the table, and a great book, and a bottle, and at the head of it all, a gray coat, a sword on the table, a long thin sallow face, and a pen, a man of forty with the look of an usher: Maillard. A prisoner stands in front of the table, between two men with pikes, with his hat in his hand. They are reading something. It is the register of the gaol.
It is the young lieutenant. You know him as soon as you see him. It is the same too young face, and the same gray coat, that you saw on the tenth of August in the corridor of the Cour des Princes, with a roll of paper. He is the Swiss. He has been in a cell for three weeks. He has been, since the tenth, in the Abbaye, with some of his regiment, and the others have been gone, in the afternoon, by the gate. He stands very straight. He has that blank, polite, rather dazed air of a person who has had very little sleep and who is waiting for the end of a sermon.
Maillard asks him whether he fired on the people. He says that he has his orders. Maillard asks him what they were. He says that they were not to fire first, and that they were, in that case, obeyed.
?if b3.palace == "warned"
  You think of the thick fair captain, in the lamp of the stair, who said that he would remember it. You think that it is the thing that he said.
?elif b3.palace == "carried"
  There is an old woman, in the crowd, behind the pikes, in a gray gown, who was brought out by the river gate. She has her hands in her apron. She is looking at the lieutenant, and her mouth is working, and nobody has noticed.
?end
?if b3.baptiste == "went"
  Somebody in the crowd, a young man in a torn sleeve, pushes forward to see. He is gone before you can be certain.
?end
The table has not decided. There is a pause, and a murmur, and the pikes have not moved. It will be done, in the next three minutes, one way or the other. You can see which way the table inclines, and it is a thing that you cannot be certain of.
:the_table
* Go to the table, and speak for him
  $ b3.abbaye = "spoke"
  $ standing += 2
  $ humanity += 2
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_abbaye")
  It is the oddest speech that you have ever made. You make it with your hat in your hand. You say that you are a citizen of the section, and a printer's friend, and that you were at the Tuileries on the night of the ninth, and that you saw the whole of it, and that the young man at the table is not guilty of any act, and that his orders were what he has said. You say that it is a Swiss regiment, and that it has been sold, twice, by its own officers, and that the citizens do not wish it to be said, in the morning, that they were not just. Maillard looks at you, and at the lamp, and at the book. It is a long time. He says that the citizen speaks well. He says that the table has been a good deal pressed. He says, to the man in the leather apron, in a tone of immense weariness, that the prisoner is acquitted. The man shouts. The pikes go up. They carry the lieutenant out, between them, on their shoulders, with a cry of long live the nation, through the gate, into the street, in the light of the torches. He does not seem to understand. He looks back at you, over somebody's head, with his round young face, as though he had left something behind. Maillard writes, in the book, with his pen. He does not look up. He says, as you go, to the room in general, that the citizen's name has been noted.
* Take him out by the back stair, with the keeper's key
  $ b3.abbaye = "took"
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing += 1
  $ do chart("paris.rebels")
  $ do discover("the_abbaye")
  There is a keeper, a stout, frightened man in a nightcap, at a door behind the stair, with a ring of keys, who has been sitting on a bench with his head in his hands since six. He does not know you. He knows what you are, in the way that a dog knows a thing that is not a dog. You say that you wish to take a prisoner out. He says that it is not possible. You say it again. It is a thing that is very easy to do and that you do not like at all. He gives you the key. You go through the passage behind the table, in the dark, and take the lieutenant by the arm, when he is turned away from the light, and put your hand on his mouth. It is not a rough thing. It takes a quarter of a minute. You are out of the little wicket, in the rue Sainte-Marguerite, in the dark, before the table has finished its pause, and you walk him quite quickly through the lanes to the river. He says nothing. He is not able to. At the Pont Royal, where the water is black, you take away your hand. He says, in German, a word that is not a thanks. It is an extremely frightened one. Behind you, somewhere, a voice says, in a great fatigue, that the prisoner has been lost.
* Stay in the street, and do not go in
  $ b3.abbaye = "stayed"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ do chart("paris.rebels")
  $ do discover("the_abbaye")
  You stand in the rue de Bussy, among the lanterns. You stand there for a very long time. You do not know, afterwards, how long. You do not go in. There is a thing that you are able to hear, through the wall, over the heads of the crowd, and the drums, and the song that somebody has begun on the pavement, which has to do with a dance. It is a short thing. It is a voice, a young one, saying, very clearly, and quite without reproach, a name that you do not know, and then the cry. It is the same cry that you have heard in every revolution. It is a good deal like a cheer. You stay where you are until the first light begins, a very long way off, over Charenton, and a boy comes round the corner with a bucket and a broom.
It is a very long night. It is the second of September, and the third, and the fourth, it will be said, and the fifth: it goes on in the Châtelet, and La Force, and the Conciergerie, and the Bicêtre, and the Salpêtrière, for four days, and the Commune sends its circular to the provinces, and by the end of it there are a thousand and some hundreds of the dead.
?if b3.crossing == "crossed"
  You think of the Prince, in the Marais, behind a blind, with a handkerchief, and a book, hearing it.
?elif b3.crossing == "pressed"
  There is a sound, before dawn, of a very slow footfall in the rue de Bussy, and a gleam of old steel in the torchlight, and a pale, tall, solitary figure at the gate, who has come, alone, in a gray coat, in the last of the dark, and stands with a sword in his hand, and does not use it. It is Brézac. He has been there, you are told, since one. He is quite white, and his scar has gone gray, and he has not been seen to speak.
?else
  There is nobody of the court in the street. It is what the line said.
?end
You are in the haven before the sun, with the stone over you. You have a smell of smoke in your coat that you do not think that you will lose.
$ do rest()
